# UI minimal copy

**Default:** do not add UI text. Extra labels, subtitles, helper paragraphs, and explanatory banners make a clinical/front-desk dashboard noisy and slower to scan under time pressure.

Unless the user asks for copy or it is **really needed**, add **no** new text.

## Really needed (allowed)

- **Form field labels** — short; required for usability and WCAG.
- **Primary actions** — short button text (`Save`, `Send`, `Cancel`, `Confirm`).
- **Errors / validation** — only when something failed or input is invalid.
- **Destructive confirms** — one short line stating what will happen (e.g. cancelling an appointment, voiding an invoice).
- **Clinical/legal disclaimers** — only when required (consent capture, Rx disclaimers).
- **Icon-only controls** — `aria-label` / `title` (short, not a paragraph).
- **AI assistant confirm cards** — the action being proposed, stated plainly; no extra framing.

## Do not add unless asked

- Page or section subtitles under an already-clear title.
- `CardDescription`, helper text under fields, "tip" callouts.
- Empty-state essays — prefer icon + one short line, or nothing.
- Inline "this lets you…" explanations.
- Duplicating data already visible (dates, names, status badges).
- Marketing-style microcopy anywhere in the dashboard — this is operational software, not a landing page.

## When building or editing UI

1. Ship layout, hierarchy, icons, and data first.
2. Add text only where the screen would be unusable without it.
3. If unsure, **omit** — the user will ask for copy when they want it.
4. When text _is_ required, follow **`human-copy.mdc`**.

This rule wins the old `design-system.md` §12 requirement that every page carry a description. `PageHeader` `description` is optional. A description that restates the title or nav label is an anti-slop **B7** fail.
